684→## Reading large ranges
685→
686→A single `getValues()` / `getFormulas()` / `getBackgrounds()` call is capped at
687→**24 blocks**, a block being 500 rows x 20 columns — roughly 12000 rows of one
688→column. Over the cap the call throws (it never silently truncates) and the error
689→tells you how to split it.
690→
691→Read in chunks and aggregate as you go, keeping the aggregate rather than the rows.
692→Do not shrink the chunk "to be safe": every read re-assembles the whole document
693→upstream, so smaller chunks multiply the cost instead of spreading it.
694→
695→## Key Constraints
696→
697→- All batch setter methods require 2D arrays with dimensions exactly matching the target `Range`
698→- `range.clearContent()` erases content and keeps styling; `range.clear()` erases content **and** styling; `range.clearFormat()` erases styling only; `sheet.clear()` / `sheet.clearContents()` do the same over the whole worksheet. `setValue(null)` writes the literal string `"null"`
699→- After `setFormula()`, verify the result and fall back if the formula errors
700→- Delete columns from back to front to avoid index shifting
701→- `groupBy` and `deduplicateRows` use **0-based** column indices
702→- `newChart()` uses **0-based** `anchorRow` and `anchorCol`; pass `-1, -1` when the user did not name a location
703→- Color values must be hex strings with a leading `#`
704→
705→## API Constraint
706→
707→- You may **ONLY** use APIs documented in this API Reference, in a reference file you have read (e.g. the `charts-visual` / `charts-create` reference), or in currently loaded skills
708→- Do **NOT** rely on prior knowledge of any API — if it is not in the API Reference, a reference file you have read, or a loaded skill, it does not exist
709→- Calling an undocumented API will cause a runtime error
710→- If the required API does not exist, use JavaScript logic with existing APIs to achieve the goal (e.g., sort data in JS array then write back with setValues). Do NOT guess or invent API methods.
